    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d680-a1">

                <pb facs="0653" n="555" sameAs="frus:frus1900/pg555"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1 in No. 231.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Lord Salisbury to Mr.
                            Choate.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Foreign Office</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1900-01-10"><hi rendition="#italic">January 10,
                        1900</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. Choate</hi>: Our view is that foodstuffs,
                    with a hostile destination, can be considered contraband of war only if they are
                    supplies for the enemy’s forces. It is not sufficient that they are capable of
                    being so used; it must be shown that this was in fact their destination at the
                    time of the seizure.</p>
                <p>Believe me, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Salisbury.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
